gm-method (blanket "remove all comments" requests need scoping, not blind execution): when a user asks to strip comments/history that may encode unverified assumptions, ask which class survives (pure WHAT-restating comments vs load-bearing WHY-rationale for driver quirks/race conditions/invariants) before executing a destructive repo-wide transform -- the answer changes the entire operation from "delete everything" to "delete most, shrink the rest to one line." A ~210-file, ~40k-line codebase-wide strip is exactly the shape a parallel Workflow (one agent per directory cluster, each self-verifying via node --check, paired verify-agents as a second pass) should handle rather than manual file-by-file edits -- 8-16 agents finished a task that would have taken hours of serial editing in under 25 minutes. Also: a large destructive workflow running for many minutes will very likely race with OTHER concurrent sessions writing to the same repo/PRD store -- expect several rounds of "unexpected file modified, not by me" after CONSOLIDATE, and the correct response is to inspect each diff, confirm it's legitimate concurrent work (not corruption), and commit-and-push it alongside your own rather than reverting or ignoring it.